<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ashikaga Mitsukane</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1378">1378</a>–<a href="/years/1409">1409</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ashikaga Mitsukane was the third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> kubo, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s governor-general of the Kanto, from 1398 to 1409. Like his father Ujimitsu he resented the authority of the shogun in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> and was suspected of plotting with the rebel Ouchi Yoshihiro in 1399. He was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-mochiuji">Ashikaga Mochiuji</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashikaga-mitsukane.jpg" alt="Ashikaga Mitsukane" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Urashimataro · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zuisenji_Main_Hall_Kamakura.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ashikaga Mitsukane was a warrior of the Nanboku-chō period and the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-ujimitsu">Ashikaga Ujimitsu</a>, the second Kantō kubō, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a>'s deputy, of the Kamakura-fu. He succeeded his father at the age of 21 when Ujimitsu died during an epidemic. Like his father, and like those who came after him, he aspired more or less openly to the shogunate itself and never obtained it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1399, the year after he took office, he sent Mitsunao and Mitsusada to the Sasagawa Gosho and the Inamura Gosho in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutsu-province">Mutsu Province</a> to steady the region, which with Dewa Province had been granted to his father as a reward for supporting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> against the Yamana clan. He saw the area as important for control of the whole Kantō. He stayed in southern Mutsu himself from August of that year for four months, which greatly strengthened the backing of the Yūki Shirakawa family.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouchi-yoshihiro">Ōuchi Yoshihiro</a> rose in Izumi in the Ōei Rebellion of the same year, allied with the Southern Dynasty, Mitsukane planned to join him in the hope of replacing his relative, the shogun Ashikaga Yoshimochi. Ōuchi was quickly defeated, and partly on the advice of Uesugi Norisada, Mitsukane turned back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a>. Having given Ōuchi no actual help, he could feign innocence before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshimitsu">Ashikaga Yoshimitsu</a>, the retired shogun who still held power, and an open clash with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> was avoided.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In a ganbun, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a> prayer left at Mishima Taisha in Izu, he admitted attacking superior forces, thanked Uesugi Norisada and swore never to revolt again. In 1400 he swore loyalty to Yoshimitsu, and peace between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> held until his death. In 1402 he sent Uesugi Ujinori, the future Uesugi Zenshū, against a rising led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/date-masamune">Date Masamune</a> in southern and central Mutsu. A rumour went round Kyoto that he was insane; whether it had any basis is unclear. He died of natural causes at 32 and was buried at Zuisen-ji in Kamakura.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ashikaga Mitsukane” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Successors Ujimitsu, <b>Mitsukane</b> and Mochiuji resented Kyoto's authority, and tension with the Uesugi deputies led to the Uesugi Zenshu revolt of 1416.</blockquote><a href="/state/kamakura-fu">Kamakura-fu</a></li></ul></section>
